#%PAM-1.0
# The stack a TYPED PASSWORD goes through: greetd's `general.service`, used for the
# session the greeter asks it to start. This is the one that decides whether the
# person standing at the login screen gets in.
#
# It is `pam_unix` and not `pam_permit`, which is a change of behaviour rather than
# of wording. Until the greeter shipped, this file was the autologin stack as well,
# and it permitted everything - so had the greeter been wired up against it, every
# password would have opened the machine and the screen would have looked exactly
# the same either way. The autologin path now has its own `greetd-autologin` file
# with the permissive stack, so nothing about the boot depends on this one.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, and it is the honest one: this image's `arlen` user has no password
# (`useradd` leaves the shadow field `!`), so `pam_unix` refuses every login here.
# Boot still auto-logs in; logging out lands on a greeter nobody can pass. That is
# a locked machine rather than an open one, and setting a password is a decision
# for whoever installs it - the installer and the onboarding flow are where it
# belongs, not a default baked into an image.
#
# The account stack stays `pam_unix` too: an expired or disabled account is exactly
# what it is for, and greetd runs `acct_mgmt` on this path.
#
# systemd-homed will add `pam_systemd_home.so` above `pam_unix` when profiles land
# (that is the module that releases the LUKS key from the password). Self-contained
# on purpose, like its neighbours.
auth       required pam_unix.so
account    required pam_unix.so
password   required pam_unix.so
session    optional pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session    required pam_loginuid.so
session    required pam_permit.so
session    optional pam_systemd.so
